lesson physics-a-loop-with-a-break v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=4 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A loop with a break in it"
  goal "Spot the circuit that will not light, and say what is wrong with it."

  look {
    scene 35x13 {
      row two at=canvas(1, 0) space=2 align=start {
        series whole cells=2
        series broken cells=2 loose=1
      }
    }
    say """
      These two circuits have the same cells and the same bulb. In the second one a clip has come
      off the cell, so the loop has a gap and the bulb stays dark. A bulb lights only when the wire
      goes all the way round, from the cell and back again, with nothing open and nothing loose.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.will-it-light count=3 seed=2
    practice physics.why-dark count=3 seed=4
    practice physics.which-dark count=2 seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.fix-it f=2 c=2 n=1 easy-f=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.two-faults c=0 l=1 e=0 easy-l=0 hard-e=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Follow the wire round. An open switch, a loose clip or no cell, and the bulb stays dark."
  }

  grown-ups """
    When a real circuit will not work, the order the child checks things in is the skill: the
    switch, then every clip, then the cell. Let them find a fault you have made on purpose before
    you ever tell them where it is. The last question has more than one fault, which is what real
    circuits usually have, and finding the first one is not the end of the job.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the faults are an open switch or a missing cell, both plain to see, and the last circuit has one thing wrong."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the faults include a loose wire, which is found only by following the loop, and the last circuit has three things wrong."
  }
}
